      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 12,
      "parent_id": 3,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 1,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 12,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Humor does not translate well because it often relies on double-meaning or a subtle play on word choice, pronunciation, or context. These issues are further exacerbated in areas where space is a premium (as frequent on social media platforms), often leading to usage and development of shorthand, in-jokes, and self-reference. Thus, building a system to classify the humor of tweets is a difficult task. However, with transfer-learning and the Fast.ai library, we can build a high quality classifier in a foreign language. Our system outperforms a Naive Bayes Support Vector Machine (NBSVM) baseline, which is frequently considered a \"strong baseline\" for many Natural Language Processing (NLP) related tasks (see Wang et al BIBREF0 ).",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 13,
      "parent_id": 3,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 1,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 13,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Rather than hand-crafted language features, we have taken an \"end to end\" approach building from the raw text to a final model that achieves the tasks as presented. Our paper lays out the details of the system and our code can be found in a GitHub repository for use by other researchers to extend the state of the art in sentiment analysis.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 14,
      "parent_id": 0,
      "type": "NodeType.TITLE",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 2,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 14,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Task and Dataset Description",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 15,
      "parent_id": 14,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 2,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 15,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "The Humor Analysis based on Human Annotation (HAHA) 2019 BIBREF1 competition asked for analysis of two tasks in the Spanish language based on a corpus of publicly collected data described in Castro et al. BIBREF2 :",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 16,
      "parent_id": 14,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 2,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 16,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "The HAHA dataset includes labeled data for 24,000 tweets and a test set of 6,000 tweets (80%/20% train/test split.) Each record includes the raw tweet text (including accents and emoticons), a binary humor label, the number of votes for each of five star ratings and a “Funniness Score” that is the average of the 1 to 5 star votes cast. Examples and data can be found on the CodaLab competition webpage.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 17,
      "parent_id": 0,
      "type": "NodeType.TITLE",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 3,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 17,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "System Description",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 18,
      "parent_id": 17,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 3,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 18,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "We modify the method of Universal Langage Model Fine-tuning for Text Classification (ULMFiT) presented in Howard and Ruder BIBREF3 . The primary steps are:",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 19,
      "parent_id": 17,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 3,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 19,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Below we will give more detail on each step and the parameters used to generate our system.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 20,
      "parent_id": 0,
      "type": "NodeType.TITLE",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 4,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 20,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Additional Data",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 21,
      "parent_id": 20,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 4,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 21,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "We collected a corpus for our LM based on Spanish Twitter using tweepy run for three 4-hour sessions and collecting any tweet with any of the terms 'el','su','lo','y' or 'en'. We excluded retweets to minimize repeated examples in our language model training. In total, we collected 475,143 tweets - a data set is nearly 16 times larger than the text provided by the competition alone. The frequency of terms, punctuation and vocabulary used on Twitter can be quite different from the standard Wikipedia corpus that is often used to train an LM from scratch.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 22,
      "parent_id": 20,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 4,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 22,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "In the fine-tuning step, we combined the train and test text data without labels from the contest data.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 23,
      "parent_id": 0,
      "type": "NodeType.TITLE",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 5,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 23,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Cleaning",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 24,
      "parent_id": 23,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 5,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 24,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "We applied a list of default cleanup functions in sequence (see list below). They are close to the standard clean-up included in the Fast.ai library with the addition of one function for the Twitter dataset. Cleanup of data is key to expressing information in a compact way so that the LM can use the relevant data when trying to predict the next word in a sequence.",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
      "index_id": 25,
      "parent_id": 23,
      "type": "NodeType.TEXT",
      "meta_info": {
        "file_name": null,
        "file_path": null,
        "page_idx": 5,
        "page_path": null,
        "pdf_id": 25,
        "pdf_para_block": null,
        "img_path": null,
        "image_width": 0,
        "image_height": 0,
        "caption": null,
        "footnote": null,
        "table_body": null,
        "content": "Replace more than 3 repetitions of the same character (ie. grrrreat becomes g xxrep r 4 eat)",
        "title_level": -1
      },
      "summary": ""
    },
    {
